    <main class="container py-8">
        <section id="portainer-overview" class="bg-white p-8 rounded-lg shadow-md mb-8">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Portainer: The Easy-to-Use Docker Management UI</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                Portainer is a lightweight, open-source management UI for Docker. It provides a user-friendly graphical interface to manage your Docker containers, images, networks, and volumes directly from your web browser. Instead of remembering complex Docker CLI commands, Portainer lets you visualize and control your entire Docker environment with ease.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                Within the PiSelfhosting project, Portainer significantly simplifies the day-to-day administration of your self-hosted services, making Docker accessible even for those new to containerization.
            </p>
        </section>

        ---

        <section id="portainer-functionality" class="bg-white p-8 rounded-lg shadow-md mb-8">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Detailed Functionality</h2>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">Key Features:</h3>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-gray-700">
                <li><strong>Dashboard Overview:</strong> Get a quick glance at your Docker environment's health, running containers, and resource usage.</li>
                <li><strong>Container Management:</strong> Easily start, stop, restart, remove, and inspect individual containers. View logs, attach to containers, and manage their settings.</li>
                <li><strong>Image Management:</strong> Pull new images, build images, and manage existing ones.</li>
                <li><strong>Network Management:</strong> Create, inspect, and remove Docker networks.</li>
                <li><strong>Volume Management:</strong> Create, inspect, and remove Docker volumes, and even browse their contents.</li>
                <li><strong>Stacks (Docker Compose):</strong> Deploy and manage entire multi-service applications using Docker Compose files directly within the UI.</li>
                <li><strong>User Management:</strong> Set up users and teams with role-based access control.</li>
                <li><strong>Templates:</strong> Deploy common applications from predefined templates.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">PiSelfhosting Integration:</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                The PiSelfhosting project sets up Portainer to give you immediate visual control over your Docker setup:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-gray-700">
                 <li><strong>Docker Compose Template:</strong> The <code class="code">docker-compose.yml.template</code> file in <code class="code">/home/PiSelfhosting/scripts/templates/portainer/</code> defines the Portainer service. Key aspects include:
                    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-2 text-gray-700">
                        <li>Image: <code class="code">portainer/portainer-ce:latest</code> (the official Portainer Community Edition image).</li>
                        <li>Ports: Mapping for the web UI (default 9000).</li>
                        <li>Volumes:
                            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-1 mt-1 text-gray-700">
                                <li><code class="code">/var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock</code>: This is the **most critical volume mapping** for Portainer. It allows Portainer to communicate directly with the Docker daemon on your host system, enabling it to manage your Docker environment.</li>
                                <li><code class="code">portainer_data:/data</code>: A named volume to ensure persistent storage of Portainer's own configuration, user accounts, and settings.</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-6 mb-2">Initial Setup & Usage:</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                After deploying Portainer, there's a quick one-time setup:
            </p>
            <ol class="list-decimal list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-gray-700">
                <li><strong>Access the UI:</strong> Open your web browser and navigate to <code class="code">http://[your-pi-ip]:9000</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Create Admin User:</strong> On your first access, you'll be prompted to create an administrator username and password. Choose strong credentials.</li>
                <li><strong>Connect to Docker Environment:</strong> Select "Get Started" to connect to your local Docker environment (which is automatically detected via <code class="code">/var/run/docker.sock</code>).</li>
                <li><strong>Explore:</strong> Once connected, you can browse your "Local" environment to see all your PiSelfhosting containers, networks, and volumes.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                Portainer is an incredibly powerful tool. Take some time to explore its interface and features to streamline your Docker management.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section id="portainer-troubleshooting" class="bg-white p-8 rounded-lg shadow-md mb-8">
            <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mb-4">Troubleshooting</h2>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
                If you encounter issues with Portainer:
            </p>
            <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 mt-4 text-gray-700">
                <li><strong>Check Portainer logs:</strong> Use <code class="code">docker logs piselfhosting-portainer</code> for any errors during startup.</li>
                <li><strong>Verify Port Accessibility:</strong> Ensure port 9000 is not blocked by a firewall or used by another service on your host.</li>
                <li><strong>Docker Socket Mapping:</strong> Confirm that the <code class="code">/var/run/docker.sock</code> volume is correctly mounted. Without it, Portainer cannot communicate with Docker.</li>
                <li><strong>Docker Daemon Status:</strong> Ensure the Docker daemon itself is running on your Raspberry Pi: <code class="code">sudo systemctl status docker</code>.</li>
                <li><strong>Official Documentation:</strong> The <a href="https://docs.portainer.io/user/getting-started/quick-start" target="_blank">official Portainer documentation</a> is an excellent resource for detailed setup and troubleshooting.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>
